   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.7",
    "topic_title": "Proteins",
    "stem": "A soluble protein folds so that amino acids with hydrophobic R groups cluster in its interior, away from water. A single change in the amino acid sequence replaces one of these buried hydrophobic amino acids with an amino acid that has a charged (ionic) R group. All other amino acids are unchanged. Which prediction best describes the most likely effect on the protein?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The protein is unaffected, because the peptide bonds linking the amino acids remain intact and only one amino acid out of many was changed.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap treats the backbone as the sole source of shape and assumes one change is too small to matter. Folding depends on R-group interactions, so one charged R group in a hydrophobic core can alter the whole three-dimensional shape. Fix: Ask which R-group interactions the new amino acid can no longer form, then trace how that changes folding."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The peptide bonds on either side of the new amino acid break, splitting the polypeptide into two separate fragments.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses a change in folding with breaking covalent bonds. Peptide bonds are broken by hydrolysis, not by a different R group; the chain stays intact while its folding interactions change. Fix: Check whether a change affects covalent peptide bonds or the weaker R-group interactions that shape folding."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The protein's function is unchanged, because the substitution is buried in the interior, away from the surface region that binds its target.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes only surface amino acids matter for function. Interior hydrophobic packing stabilizes the entire tertiary structure, so disrupting the core can reshape the binding surface too. Fix: Ask how a change anywhere in the fold could alter the overall shape before ruling out an effect on function."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The charged R group disrupts hydrophobic interactions in the interior, altering tertiary folding and overall shape, which can impair the protein's ability to bind its target and reduce its function.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "This traces the full chain: the sequence change introduces a charged R group into a hydrophobic core, which disrupts the interactions that stabilize tertiary structure. The protein's three-dimensional shape changes, and because function depends on shape, binding to its target is likely impaired."
     }
    ]
   },
